course: dictionary
lesson: 3273
title: Erode: wearing away slowly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Erode means to wear something away slowly, bit by bit, until it becomes smaller or weaker. Think of a cliff by the sea: each wave takes a tiny piece, and over many years the cliff gets smaller.
explain: «Australia's Twelve Apostles are limestone stacks that are slowly collapsing as the sea erodes them.» The clues are slowly collapsing and the sea. The sea is wearing the stacks away, so erodes means wears away.
explain: The word also works for ideas. Trust can be eroded when someone keeps letting you down: it gets slowly weaker, not all at once. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If "wears away slowly" still makes sense, you have it.
fact: Erode means to wear away slowly, as wind, water or ice rub away rock and soil.
fact: Erode can also mean to make something slowly weaker or smaller, like trust.
fact: The noun for this process is erosion.
fact: «The Holderness coast in Yorkshire is one of Europe's fastest-eroding coasts, losing about 1-2 metres a year.»
fact: «Clearing forests and ploughing can make soil erode many times faster than natural rates.»
exercise: «The sea slowly erodes the cliffs.» What does erodes mean here? | Freezes solid | Paints in bright colours | *Wears away slowly | Builds up quickly
exercise: «Clearing forests can make soil erode many times faster.» What does this sentence tell us? | Forests grow faster | Soil stops moving forever | Soil turns into a hard rock | *Soil is worn away more quickly
exercise: Which word is the noun form of erode? | Erodes | Eroded | *Erosion | Erode
test: «Rivers erode valleys over thousands of years.» Which phrase means the same as erode here? | Shine brightly | *Slowly wear away | Stay the same | Fill up
test: «Lies can erode trust between friends.» What happens to trust when it is eroded? | It becomes louder | It turns into stone | It grows bigger | *It gets slowly weaker
exam: «Waves erode the cliff over many years.» Which word is closest in meaning to erode? | Freeze | Polish | Build | *Wear away
